      <p>Personal injury is where Google Ads is at its most expensive and its most valuable at the same time. Clicks can cost more here than in almost any other industry, because a single signed case can be worth a great deal and many firms compete for the same searches.</p>
      <p>That combination punishes waste harder than anywhere else. A few hundred wrong clicks can use a month's budget, and an unanswered call can lose a case worth more than the whole campaign.</p>

      <h2>Structure by case type</h2>
      <p>"Personal injury lawyer" is the obvious keyword and often the least efficient. People search for what happened to them, and each case type has its own value and its own competition:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Car accidents, the largest volume in most markets</li>
        <li>Truck and commercial vehicle accidents, often higher value</li>
        <li>Motorcycle and pedestrian accidents</li>
        <li>Slip and fall and premises liability</li>
        <li>Wrongful death, handled with particular care in wording</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Separate campaigns or ad groups by case type let the budget follow the cases the firm actually wants to take, with ads and landing pages that speak to that situation rather than to injury law in general.</p>

      <h2>Searches that look good and are not</h2>
      <p>Some searches mention injury and accidents but come from people who will never become clients, and at personal injury prices each one hurts:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Job searches: paralegal jobs, lawyer salary, law school</li>
        <li>Research searches such as "how much is my case worth" or "average settlement", which can be worth reaching but rarely at full bids</li>
        <li>Practice areas you do not handle, such as workers' compensation or criminal defence, if they are not yours</li>
        <li>Locations where your lawyers are not licensed to practise</li>
      </ul>
      <p>On the other hand, "free consultation" is a buying search for personal injury, where contingency fees are normal, so it should not be blocked. <a href="/learn/how-to-find-negative-keywords/">How to find negative keywords</a> covers the method.</p>

      <h2>Intake decides the result</h2>
      <p>No campaign can save a lead that nobody answers. People who have just been injured often call the first firm that picks up.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Calls answered around the clock, or ads scheduled only for the hours they are</li>
        <li>Forms and chats responded to within minutes, not hours</li>
        <li>Call recording or notes, so lead quality can be judged honestly</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Often the fastest improvement in a law firm account is not in Google Ads at all. It is in how quickly and how well the firm responds.</p>

      <h2>Measure signed cases, not leads</h2>
      <p>A cost per lead that looks excellent means nothing if those leads do not sign. Personal injury needs a chain of measurement: lead, qualified consultation, signed case.</p>
      <p>We record the source of every lead, then feed back which ones became signed cases from your intake or case management system, even weeks later. That shows cost per signed case by campaign and case type, and lets bidding learn from cases rather than from enquiries. The <a href="/tools/metrics-calculator/">metrics calculator</a> works out the cost per case you can afford.</p>

      <h2>Local Services Ads for lawyers</h2>
      <p>Where Local Services Ads are available for your practice area and location, they appear above the normal results with a Google verification badge and charge per lead. They work well alongside Search, which keeps control over case types and wording. Measure the two separately, both on signed cases.</p>

      <h2>Advertising rules</h2>
      <p>Lawyers advertise under the professional rules of their state or country, which can cover claims about past results, testimonials and terms like specialist or expert. Ad copy and landing pages should be checked against those rules before they run, alongside Google's own policies.</p>

